Communication Protocols 

1. What Even Is a Communication Protocol?
Analogy: Two people trying to talk on a walkie-talkie need to agree on a few things first —
who talks first, how fast to speak, how you know a sentence has ended, and what to do if you
didn't hear clearly. That agreement is the protocol.
Chips are no different. Before two devices can exchange data, they need to agree on:
Who talks first — is there a "master" device in charge, or do peers talk freely?
How fast — clock speed / baud rate
How to mark the start/end of a message (framing)
How to confirm the message got through (acknowledgement, checksums)
Keep this as the opening slide — it reframes "protocol" from a scary word into something
intuitive before the acronyms start.
2. The Three Practical Buckets
1. Wired, short-distance — chip talking to a sensor/display sitting right next to it: UART,
I2C, SPI
2. Wireless, device-to-device or device-to-network: WiFi, Bluetooth/BLE, Zigbee,
LoRa
3. Application/cloud layer — once you're already on WiFi and need to talk to the
internet: HTTP, MQTT
This 3-bucket framing is a good structural slide — everything below hangs off it.
3. Wired Serial Protocols (sensor ↔ microcontroller)
UART — the private phone line
Analogy: A landline call between exactly two people. One wire to talk, one wire to listen. No
shared clock — both sides just have to agree in advance how fast they'll speak (the baud
rate), like agreeing "we'll both talk at normal speed, not fast."
Pins: TX, RX (cross-connected — TX of one goes to RX of the other)
Strictly point-to-point: only 2 devices, ever
Use cases: ESP32 ↔ laptop (Serial Monitor / flashing), GPS modules, AT-command
Bluetooth/GSM modules
I2C — the office intercom
Analogy: One shared intercom line in an office. Everyone's plugged into the same two
wires, but each person has an extension number. The "master" calls out an extension, and
only that device responds — everyone else stays silent.
Pins: SDA (data) + SCL (clock), shared by all devices on the bus
Each slave device has a unique address
Big win: many devices, only 2 wires
Trade-off: slower than SPI
Use cases: hooking up multiple sensors at once (temp sensor + IMU + OLED display,
all sharing SDA/SCL)
SPI — the private courier service
Analogy: Instead of one shared line, you send a dedicated courier to each recipient — fast
and reliable, but you need a new courier (wire) for every new recipient.
Pins: MOSI, MISO, SCK shared, plus one CS (Chip Select) wire per device
Full-duplex — can send and receive at the same time → fast
Trade-off: wiring grows with every device added
Use cases: SD cards, TFT/OLED displays, high-speed sensors
