4. Wireless Protocols
WiFi — the highway
Analogy: A highway — huge bandwidth, gets you anywhere (including straight to the
internet), but it's a gas guzzler and needs infrastructure (a router) to exist.
High speed, direct path to the internet
Power-hungry — bad choice for a coin-cell sensor meant to last months
Use case: ESP32 pushing sensor data to a cloud dashboard, OTA updates
Bluetooth / BLE — the walkie-talkie
Analogy: Two friends with walkie-talkies standing near each other — short range, but
quick to "pair up" and doesn't drain the battery fast.
Classic Bluetooth: audio streaming, older devices
BLE (Bluetooth Low Energy): tiny, infrequent bursts of data, sips power — built for
wearables/sensors
Use case: ESP32 ↔ phone app control, fitness bands, proximity tags
Zigbee & LoRa — mesh & long-range (brief mention)
Analogies:
Zigbee = a bucket brigade — each node passes the message to its neighbor, forming a
self-healing mesh that can blanket a whole building with low-power nodes.
LoRa = shouting across a valley — kilometers of range, but you can only shout short,
occasional messages (very low data rate).
Use cases: Zigbee → smart home mesh (e.g. Philips Hue); LoRa → utility meters /
agriculture sensors spread over large areas
