5. Talking to the Cloud — Application Layer
(Only relevant once the ESP32 is already on WiFi and needs to reach a server)
HTTP/HTTPS — ordering at a counter
Analogy: You walk up to a counter, state your full request, and wait for the full response —
every single time, with no memory of the last visit.
Simple, universal, but "heavier" for constant small updates
Use case: occasional readings, calling a RESTAPI, fetching data from a server
MQTT — the newspaper subscription
Analogy: Devices "publish" updates to a topic (like a newspaper publishing an edition), and
everyone "subscribed" to that topic gets it instantly — no need to keep asking "anything
new?"
Lightweight, built specifically for IoT — tiny packets, low bandwidth, low power
Works via a central broker; devices publish/subscribe to topics rather than talking to
each other directly
Use case: live sensor dashboards, home-automation triggers ("if temp > 30°C → turn
fan ON")
CoAP (one-line mention)
Like HTTP, but on a diet — same request/response feel, built tiny for constrained devices.
6. Putting It Together — a Real ESP32 Data Flow
Sensor --(I2C)--> ESP32 --(WiFi)--> MQTT Broker --(MQTT)--> Phone App /
Dashboard
Concrete example to narrate live: a temperature sensor talks to the ESP32 over I2C → ESP32
connects to WiFi → publishes the reading to a topic like home/livingroom/temp → a phone
app subscribed to that topic shows a live graph, no polling needed.
